621:
In addition to the nuisances declared in section 8.8.3, the judge may determine that a dwelling, building, structure, or property is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use if they find that conditions exist in such building, dwelling, or structure or on such property which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety, or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building, structure or property; or the occupants of neighborhood dwellings, buildings, structures or property, or of other residents of such city. Such conditions include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing):
(1) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents, or other calamities;
(2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light, or sanitary facilities;
(3) Dilapidation;
(4) Disrepair;
(5) Structural defects; and
(6) Uncleanliness.
In the event the judge or board determines that a dwelling, building, structure, or property is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use, the judge or board may, following notice and a hearing, order that such dwelling, building, structure or property be vacated and secured until repairs, demolition, clean-up or other required abatement is accomplished.

622:
The municipal court judge may exercise such powers as may be necessary or convenient to carry out and effectuate the purpose and provisions of this article including the following powers in addition to others herein granted, to:
(a) Investigate property conditions in the city in order to determine which dwellings, buildings, structures or property are unfit for human habitation or are unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use;
(b) Administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;
(c) Enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations; provided, however, that such entries shall be made in such manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession;
(d) Appoint and fix the duties of such officers, agents, and employees as necessary to carry out the purposes of this article; and
(e) Delegate any functions and powers under this article to such officers and agents as necessary.
State Law reference— Legislative findings regarding unfit buildings, O.C.G.A. § 41-2-7.

636:
Enforcement officer, as used in this article, shall mean any City of Roswell police officer or any employee of the City of Roswell authorized by the city administrator or the city administrator's designee.
Loud or unruly conduct, as used in this section, includes any of the following conduct if in violation of any provision of this Code or State law:
(1) Any noise in violation of section 8.8.3(s) of this Code;
(2) Obstruction of a street or public right-of-way, including a sidewalk;
(3) Public intoxication or drinking in public;
(4) The service of alcoholic beverages to minors;
(5) Possession and/or consumption of alcohol by minors;
(6) Assault, battery, fights, domestic violence or other disturbances of the peace;
(7) The sale or service of alcoholic beverages without a required State license;
(8) Vandalism or destruction of property;
(9) Litter;
(10) Urinating or defecating in public;
(11) Trespassing; or indecent exposure visible from neighboring residences or the streets adjoining the property at which the gathering is taking place.
Loud or unruly gathering means a gathering of persons at any residence within a residential neighborhood or a property adjoining a residential neighborhood where loud or unruly conduct occurs at the residence or within five hundred (500) feet of the residence, and which threatens or interferes with the public health, safety or welfare, or the comfortable enjoyment of life and property.
Minor means any person under twenty-one (21) years of age.
Owner means any person who owns the residence where a loud or unruly gathering occurs.
Person or entity means an individual or any firm, association, organization, partnership, trust, business, corporation, company or legal entity.
Residence means any "residential building" includes any dwelling, garage, carport, walkway, yard, patio, deck, or other structure or area on the lot on which the residential building is located.
Residential neighborhood refers to any land use zone designated as residential in Article 3 of the Roswell, Georgia Unified Development Code.
Responsible party means:
(1) Any person or entity who rents, leases or otherwise is in charge of the residence where a loud or unruly gathering occurs; or
(2) Any person or entity who organizes or sponsors a loud or unruly gathering at a residence.
(Ord. No. 2023-07-12, § 1, Added 07/10/2023)

